<p>Among its smart home product announcements on Tuesday, Apple <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/10/apple-announces-the-next-generation-of-homepod-mini/">announced the new HomePod mini 2</a>, priced at $149 and powered by an S10 chip (introduced in the Apple Watch Series 10). Curiously, its banner feature—support for Siri AI—will not be available when the product ships on October 30.</p>
<p>According to Apple, Siri AI will come to the HomePod mini 2 &#8220;in a future software update.&#8221; When it does, Apple&#8217;s press release hints that it will be able to tap into personal context when paired with an Apple Intelligence-capable iPhone or iPad. When there&#8217;s no such device available, it&#8217;ll still have access to broader Siri AI functionality, but won&#8217;t have access to the treasure trove of data that rides with your device.</p>
<p>Of course, since home accessories are the order of the day, Apple has touted the HomePod mini 2&#8217;s integration with the broader home experience. Like its predecessor, you can use it to control home devices via voice commands. Apple&#8217;s also supporting a new four-speaker surround sound setup, when four HomePod minis are connected to the new Apple TV 4K.</p>
<p>Apple also continues to tinker with that little glowing circle at the top of the HomePod, that&#8217;s not quite a display but is&#8230; something. The new Sound Glow feature turns it into a visualizer that is based on the sound of the currently playing song, and its color scheme will match the song&#8217;s associated album art.</p>
